                    <geom mesh="link7_c" class="collision"/>
                    <!-- Replaced the stock Franka parallel-jaw gripper with a Robotiq 2F-85
                         (mujoco_menagerie/robotiq_2f85/2f85.xml) -- DROID's real robot is a
                         Franka arm + Robotiq 2F-85, not the stock Franka hand (confirmed via
                         web search: "The robot arm is equipped with a Robotiq 2F-85 gripper",
                         DROID paper / droid-dataset.github.io; and via sim_eval/robots/
                         franka_droid.py, which builds its sim agent from a
                         "Franka FR3 + Robotiq 2F-85 gripper" URDF). MolmoAct2-DROID's gripper
                         action/state dimension was trained against this hardware, so sending it
                         a proxy Panda-finger reading (as before) is itself a domain-gap source
                         independent of any network/latency/resolution issue.

                         pos="0 0 0.107" is the standard Franka flange offset from link7 (kept
                         unchanged -- Franka publishes this as the fixed link7-to-flange
                         distance, and the stock hand's own "0 0 0.107" already used exactly this
                         number). Dropped the stock hand's quat="0.9238795 0 0 -0.3826834"
                         (a 45-degree twist), though: that twist aligns the flange with the
                         *stock Franka gripper's* own mesh/finger-axis convention specifically,
                         not a universal mounting requirement -- a different gripper bolted to
                         the same flange has no reason to share it. Using identity orientation
                         here instead (gripper mounts flush, no added twist). Verified by
                         rendering: the 2F-85 hangs cleanly forward of link7 with no mesh
                         interpenetration, and its fingers open along the same local Y axis the
                         stock hand's fingers did, so the wrist camera's "forward = local Z"
                         convention below still holds. -->
                    <body name="hand" pos="0 0 0.107" quat="1 0 0 0">
                      <!-- base_mount + base, verbatim from 2f85.xml's own body tree (just the
                           class/material renames noted above), attached directly at the flange
                           frame with zero additional offset -- base_mount is specifically the
                           mounting-plate body designed to bolt straight onto a robot flange. -->
                      <body name="base_mount" pos="0 0 0.007" childclass="2f85">
                        <geom class="2f85visual" mesh="base_mount" material="rq_black"/>
                        <geom class="2f85collision" mesh="base_mount"/>
                        <body name="base" pos="0 0 0.0038" quat="1 0 0 -1">
                          <inertial mass="0.777441" pos="0 -2.70394e-05 0.0354675" quat="1 -0.00152849 0 0"
                            diaginertia="0.000260285 0.000225381 0.000152708"/>
                          <geom class="2f85visual" mesh="base" material="rq_black"/>
                          <geom class="2f85collision" mesh="base"/>
                          <site name="pinch" pos="0 0 0.145" type="sphere" group="5" rgba="0.9 0.9 0.9 1" size="0.005"/>

                          <!-- Wrist camera, third iteration. Both earlier attempts (DROID's
                               verbatim mount, then a hand-derived euler="180 0 0" straight down
                               local +Z) put the camera *in line* with the pinch axis: fine for
                               seeing the workspace, but the fingers either fill the whole frame
                               up close or leave it entirely once backed off far enough to clear
                               them -- there's no single in-line distance that shows both the
                               gripper AND the object well in both open and closed states, since
                               opening/closing sweeps the fingers directly across that view.

                               Fix: mount off to the side, near the base (not out past the
                               fingertips), and let MuJoCo aim it automatically at a fixed target
                               instead of hand-deriving a tilt quaternion -- same mode="targetbody"
                               trick scene_pick_place.xml already uses for external_cam. Target is
                               "grasp_target" below, placed at the same z as the existing "pinch"
                               site (the fingertip convergence point) -- since that target is
                               fixed to the base body, not the fingers, the aim point never moves
                               when the gripper opens/closes, so both fingers stay framed on
                               either side of the object regardless of gripper state. FOV
                               unchanged (physical camera property): focal=2.8mm, sensor=3.024mm
                               -> 2*atan(3.024/(2*2.8)) ~= 56.7 degrees. -->
                          <body name="grasp_target" pos="0 0 0.145"/>
                          <camera name="wrist_cam" pos="0 -0.22 0.02" mode="targetbody" target="grasp_target" fovy="56.7"/>

                          <!-- Truly rigid variant of wrist_cam, for libero/libero_closed_loop.py.
                               ADDITIVE ON PURPOSE: a new camera name, so wrist_cam above is
                               untouched and phase3_closed_loop.py / phase4_collect_demos.py (and
                               the dataset already collected through wrist_cam) behave identically.

                               Worth being precise about what this actually fixes, because
                               PHASE5_PLAN.md's Tier-B claim -- that wrist_cam "re-aims every
                               frame", "pinning the target near center" -- is not right. Both this
                               camera and its target sit on the SAME body, so mode="targetbody"
                               yields a CONSTANT view direction in the gripper frame; measured
                               across three arm configurations the forward vector is bit-identical
                               at (0, 0.86946, 0.49401). It never re-aims. And a real bolted-on
                               wrist camera would also hold a fixed point of the gripper at a fixed
                               place in frame -- the drift that carries servo information is the
                               BALL's drift relative to the gripper, which we already have.

                               What mode="targetbody" DOES get wrong is ROLL: MuJoCo keeps the
                               camera's up-vector world-referenced, so when the gripper twists
                               about its own axis the image counter-rotates instead of rotating
                               with the hardware (measured deviation 9.6e-2 in the rotation matrix
                               while the forward vector stays exact). A bolted-on camera rolls with
                               the wrist. That is the real, if modest, mismatch.

                               xyaxes below is NOT hand-derived -- the first attempt at that got both
                               axes negated, i.e. a 180-degree roll, which renders the view upside
                               down (gripper at the bottom of frame, camera apparently staring at the
                               skybox) while every sanity check on the forward vector still passes.
                               These numbers are read directly off wrist_cam's own rotation matrix
                               expressed in this body's frame at the home keyframe:
                                   Rl = R_base^T @ R_cam;  xyaxes = (Rl[:,0], Rl[:,1])
                               so at home this camera is pixel-identical to wrist_cam, and differs
                               only in that the roll stays locked to the gripper afterwards. Same
                               pos and fovy. Re-derive the same way if the mount ever moves. -->
                          <camera name="wrist_cam_rigid" pos="0 -0.22 0.02" fovy="56.7"
                            xyaxes="-1 0 0  0 0.49401 -0.86946"/>

                          <!-- Right-hand side 4-bar linkage -->
                          <body name="right_driver" pos="0 0.0306011 0.054904">
                            <inertial mass="0.00899563" pos="2.96931e-12 0.0177547 0.00107314" quat="0.681301 0.732003 0 0"
